# BMC PLUGIN
#
#        one variable is mandatory:
#        - config: contains a snippet of code to run during node installation time

# Plugin selection based on the following search path and priority:
#
# 1 node name                 e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/node001.py
# 2 group name                e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/compute.py
# 3 manufacturer              e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell.py
# 4 default                   e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/default.py
#
# Steps 1 to 3 are each narrowed by the node's model, which is offered first:
#
#   <name><model>.py    e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dellpoweredger650.py
#   <name>/<model>.py   e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell/poweredger650.py
#   <name>/default.py   e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell/default.py
#   <name>.py           e.g. plugins/boot/bmc/dell.py

# The manufacturer and the model are DERIVED from the node's nodeinventory row,
# normalised into a filename - lowercased, non-alphanumerics dropped, and the
# first word only for the manufacturer, since vendor strings carry a company
# suffix. 'Dell Inc.' becomes dell; 'PowerEdge R650' becomes poweredger650. See
# plugins/control/README, which uses the same derivation.

# THE TIMING MATTERS HERE, and it is why this arrived after the control family.
# These plugins run WHILE a node is being provisioned, and in-band inventory is
# collected BY that provisioning - so on a node's first install there is no in-band
# snapshot to select on. The manufacturer therefore has to come from an
# out-of-band Redfish collection, which is what 'luna node refreshinventory' does.
# Collect a node's inventory before installing it and the vendor plugin applies to
# the very first install; do not, and it resolves to default.py exactly as it
# always has.

# There is deliberately no vendor-neutral 'redfish' step here, unlike the control
# family. These plugins emit a shell snippet that the install template runs on the
# node - they do not talk to a service - so there is nothing standards-based to
# fall back on.

# WHAT SHIPS, AND WHY EACH ONE EXISTS
#
#   default.py   the generic ipmitool flow: BMC network parameters and the
#                managed admin user. Every other file here builds on it.
#   dell.py      prefers racadm. iDRAC needs IPMI-over-LAN enabled explicitly.
#   lenovo.py    prefers OneCLI. An XClarity Controller ships with IPMI-over-LAN
#                switched OFF and its factory account behind a forced password
#                change, so ipmitool cannot bootstrap a fresh XCC at all.
#   hpe.py       prefers hponcfg. iLO ships with IPMI/DCMI-over-LAN switched OFF;
#                hponcfg reaches it in band through the hpilo driver, which is
#                why it can turn the network path on.
#   hp.py        HPE hardware reporting 'HP' - the same plugin as hpe.py
#   hewlett.py   HPE hardware reporting the full company name - same again
#   ibm.py       x86 'IBM' is Lenovo hardware since 2014 - the same as lenovo.py
#
# The alias files exist as FILES rather than as entries in a lookup table on
# purpose: what is supported is then visible in a directory listing, and a site
# whose hardware reports something we have not seen adds one of these itself
# rather than waiting for a table it cannot see to be extended.

# WRITING ONE
#
# Compose, do not copy. lenovo.py and hpe.py subclass default.py and prepend
# only their vendor block:
#
#     from plugins.boot.bmc.default import Plugin as DefaultPlugin
#     class Plugin(DefaultPlugin):
#         config = VENDOR_BLOCK + DefaultPlugin.config
#
# A vendor file that restates the generic bootstrap owns a private fork of it,
# and the two drift - a fix lands in one and the node runs the other.
# (dell.py predates this and still carries its own copy.)
#
# Find the tool with 'command -v <tool>' and nothing else, exactly as dell.py
# finds racadm. A hardcoded /opt/<vendor>/... path is right on the machine it
# was written on and wrong on the next one.
#
# Probe that the tool can actually TALK to the BMC before trusting it - being
# installed is not the same as being able to reach anything - and carry a
# readiness flag that any failing step clears. The generic flow then runs as it
# would have on a machine with no vendor tool at all. A vendor plugin may add
# behaviour; it may never remove any.
#
# Configure the BMC and nothing else. NTP servers, DNS servers, timezones, boot
# order and password policy are things a site decides and Luna configures
# elsewhere. tests/unit/test_bmc_vendor_plugins.py holds all of the above.
